A||||4||JohnnyK||Should there be a sort of clip here? (headlights)||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||01-23-2001||11:12 PM||||I don't know how to explain where I mean very well. The motor turns and moves this long plastic shaft that is attached to the actual headlight assembly. I'm talking about that long plastic shaft, and am wondering if there should be a sort of E-clip or something that holds that long piece onto the actual motor? It looks like that piece has a sort of plastic dowel (cylinder) shaped thing coming out of it (which fits into a hole in the motor) and there is a groove on the side, I assume a clip should go here and that is the cause of my jerky motion. Anyone know what should be there? I hope that makes sense..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||01-23-2001||11:40 PM||||Yes there should be a clip on the shaft and could well be part, if not all of your problem. An 'E' clip should do the job nicely.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||01-24-2001||09:06 AM||||What kind of clip "should" be there?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||01-24-2001||04:11 PM||||You can just use something that will fit the groove, I've found a couple 3 different retaining clips on them. I think that the factory used a clip sinilar to the clips found on the throttle cables at the throttle bodies. I don't know for sure if one from that location will work. An 'E' clip will do nicely, like I said before. OK?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||01-24-2001||05:15 PM||||Use an E clip.  There is also a small plastic bushing of sorts that goes around the post inside of the opening.  If this is gone the headlight will have a little bit of play in it when raised.  I accidentally broke the bushings in the left light on my dads 87 replacing the motor and it sits a little low.  I am going to put new ones in during our rebuild so it goes up all the way.  But we used E clips to hold it together without problem.<P>------------------<BR>John<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">     <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1987 Fiero Sport Coupe *In rebuilding*<BR>1997 Ford Probe SE<BR>AOL IM: Fformula88||||reg||1
